cpufreq: mediatek: support MT6797, and do not let one cluster sink the rest

Three changes, all of which #50 proved were needed.

1. PLATFORM DATA AND A MACHINE ENTRY.

MT6797's Vproc/Vsram rule is not the 100-200 mV window the other SoCs here
use. The vendor requires only Vsram >= Vproc and at most
MAX_DIFF_VSRAM_VPROC (300 mV) above it, with NORMAL_DIFF_VRSAM_VPROC of
100 mV as the target; proc_max_volt is the vendor's MAX_VPROC_VOLT of
1200 mV. The shifts are carried for completeness only -- this board has no
sram regulator wired to the CPUs (VSRAM is an on-die LDO array pinned at its
1200 mV maximum, which covers the whole Vproc range in use), so software
voltage tracking never runs and only proc_max_volt is consulted.

2. SKIP CPUs THIS DRIVER CANNOT MANAGE.

The probe loop runs over every possible CPU and treats any failure as fatal.
MT6797 is tri-cluster: its two Cortex-A72s are described in the device tree
but have no clock the kernel may drive -- the vendor sets that cluster's
armpll_addr to NULL and scales it through the iDVFS co-processor -- and no OPP
table here. Without this, clk_get(cpu8, "cpu") fails and the eight Cortex-A53s
lose their DVFS along with it, which is the entire problem this driver is
being enabled to fix.

A CPU with no operating-points-v2 is not this driver's to manage. On #51 that
reads:

    mtk-cpufreq mtk-cpufreq: cpu8: no operating-points-v2, not managing it
    mtk-cpufreq mtk-cpufreq: cpu9: no operating-points-v2, not managing it

3. BLOCKLIST mt6797 IN cpufreq-dt-platdev.

cpufreq-dt-platdev creates a generic cpufreq device for any SoC not on its
blocklist whose CPU nodes carry operating-points-v2. Adding OPP tables for the
A53s therefore handed the CPUs to cpufreq-dt instead of to this driver -- and
cpufreq-dt got it wrong, publishing one policy over all ten CPUs (the A72s
included) carrying the little cluster's frequency table. Every other MediaTek
SoC with a mediatek-cpufreq entry is already on that blocklist; mt6797 was
only absent because it had never had OPP tables before.

Measured on #51 with all three in place: policy0 = cpu0-3 with the LL table,
policy4 = cpu4-7 with the L table, driver mtk-cpufreq, and the PCW the
hardware holds matches the OPP arithmetic at every point tested. A fixed
integer loop gives 286 -> 502 Miter/s on the little cluster (1.755x against a
1.725x clock ratio) and 402 -> 666 Miter/s on the big-little cluster (1.657x
against 1.656x) -- the 1.72x and 1.57x this was predicted to be worth.

--- a/drivers/cpufreq/mediatek-cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/mediatek-cpufreq.c
@@ -636,10 +636,35 @@
 	}
 
 	for_each_possible_cpu(cpu) {
+		struct device *cpu_dev;
+
 		info = mtk_cpu_dvfs_info_lookup(cpu);
 		if (info)
 			continue;
 
+		/*
+		 * A CPU with no operating-points-v2 is not this driver's to
+		 * manage, and must not take the CPUs that are down with it.
+		 *
+		 * MT6797 is tri-cluster. Its two Cortex-A72s are described in
+		 * the device tree and are therefore "possible", but they have
+		 * no ARMPLL the kernel may drive -- the vendor sets that
+		 * cluster's armpll_addr to NULL and scales it through the
+		 * iDVFS co-processor instead -- and no OPP table here. Failing
+		 * the whole probe over them would leave the eight A53s pinned
+		 * at whatever the bootloader chose, which is the entire
+		 * problem this driver is being enabled to fix.
+		 */
+		cpu_dev = get_cpu_device(cpu);
+		if (cpu_dev && cpu_dev->of_node &&
+		    !of_find_property(cpu_dev->of_node, "operating-points-v2",
+				      NULL)) {
+			dev_info(&pdev->dev,
+				 "cpu%d: no operating-points-v2, not managing it\n",
+				 cpu);
+			continue;
+		}
+
 		info = devm_kzalloc(&pdev->dev, sizeof(*info), GFP_KERNEL);
 		if (!info) {
 			ret = -ENOMEM;
@@ -682,6 +707,26 @@
 	.probe		= mtk_cpufreq_probe,
 };
 
+/*
+ * MT6797's Vproc/Vsram rules differ from the 100-200 mV window most of these
+ * SoCs use: the vendor requires only Vsram >= Vproc, and at most
+ * MAX_DIFF_VSRAM_VPROC (300 mV) above it, with NORMAL_DIFF_VRSAM_VPROC of
+ * 100 mV as the target. proc_max_volt is the vendor's MAX_VPROC_VOLT.
+ *
+ * The shifts are carried for completeness only. This board has no sram
+ * regulator wired to the CPUs -- VSRAM is an on-die LDO array pinned at its
+ * 1200 mV maximum, which covers the whole Vproc range in use -- so software
+ * voltage tracking never runs and only proc_max_volt is consulted.
+ */
+static const struct mtk_cpufreq_platform_data mt6797_platform_data = {
+	.min_volt_shift = 100000,
+	.max_volt_shift = 300000,
+	.proc_max_volt = 1200000,
+	.sram_min_volt = 1000000,
+	.sram_max_volt = 1200000,
+	.ccifreq_supported = false,
+};
+
 static const struct mtk_cpufreq_platform_data mt2701_platform_data = {
 	.min_volt_shift = 100000,
 	.max_volt_shift = 200000,
@@ -738,6 +783,7 @@
 static const struct of_device_id mtk_cpufreq_machines[] __initconst = {
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt2701", .data = &mt2701_platform_data },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt2712", .data = &mt2701_platform_data },
+	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt6797", .data = &mt6797_platform_data },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt7622", .data = &mt7622_platform_data },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt7623", .data = &mt7623_platform_data },
 	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt8167", .data = &mt8516_platform_data },
